  "response": "# Likely cause of the date-boundary flakiness in the billing tests\n\nI worked only from the scan output and haven't opened `src/billing.ts`. Line references come from the scan, and the code below is a sketch of the fix, not a patch.\n\n## Most likely cause: `src/billing.ts` reads the clock seven times (crime_00009)\n\n- **What the scan found:** 5× `Date.now()` and 2× `new Date()` at lines 57, 63, 92, 198, 238, 250 and 273.\n- **Where they are:** lines 57, 63, 92, 198 and 238 fall inside `generateInvoice` (lines 50–253, crime_00033). Line 250 sits just after its swallowed-error catch, so it's in that function too.\n- **Why the scan's \"lower risk\" note doesn't rule it out:** the scan says none of these reads decide a branch; they \"record or render the reading\". That lowers the risk of wrong logic, but it's exactly the pattern behind boundary flakes:\n  1. **Reads that straddle a boundary.** If the invoice's `createdAt` comes from one read (say L57) and its period, due date or label from another (L63/L92), a run that crosses midnight or a month end gets values from two different days or months.\n  2. **The test reads its own clock.** If the test builds its expected value with its own `new Date()` / `Date.now()` and compares it to what `generateInvoice` rendered, the two reads can land on opposite sides of a day, month or year boundary. The test then fails only when CI runs near midnight UTC or at month end.\n  3. **The tests can't pin it.** With no injected clock, nothing in `src/billing.test.ts` can freeze time. The tests themselves are also weak: one makes no assertions (crime_00027) and one uses only weak matchers (crime_00030). So they're flaky without protecting much.\n\n## Things I ruled down\n\n- **`src/date.ts` has the worst date problems, but it probably isn't involved:**\n  - mixed UTC/local reads on `d` at L9 and L41 (crime_00005, high)\n  - date strings parsed with no timezone at L29 and L33 (crime_00010)\n  - `+ 86400000` day arithmetic that breaks on DST days at L52 (crime_00016)\n  - a clock read that decides a branch at L4 (crime_00001)\n\n  Its blast radius is 0 direct importers, so nothing in the repo appears to import it, `billing.ts` included. Check `grep -n \"date\" src/billing.ts src/billing.test.ts`. If either file does import it, the mixed UTC/local reads become the prime suspect. That bug only shows up when the host's UTC offset pushes a date across a day or month line, which matches \"date-boundary\" flakiness well.\n- **The `User`/`Invoice` mismatches (crime_00011/12):** the two `createdAt` declarations disagree on type, which is a real contract issue, but it's checked at compile time and wouldn't cause flaky test failures.\n- **The swallowed error in `generateInvoice` (crime_00032):** it hides email failures but has nothing to do with time.\n\n## Proposed fix\n\nInject a clock, read it once per operation, and pin it in tests:\n\n```ts\n// billing.ts\nexport type Clock = () => number;\nconst systemClock: Clock = () => Date.now();\n\nexport function generateInvoice(user: User, opts: { /* existing */ }, now: Clock = systemClock): Invoice {\n  const at = now();               // single read for the whole invoice\n  const createdAt = at;\n  const issued = new Date(at);    // derive every date from `at`, not a fresh read\n  // ...replace the other reads at L63/92/198/238/250 with `at` / `issued`\n}\n```\n\nThen make the reads derived from `at` consistently UTC (`getUTC*`, `toISOString()`), so the result doesn't depend on the host's timezone.\n\n```ts\n// billing.test.ts\nimport { vi, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\n\nit.each([\n  \"2026-01-31T23:59:59.999Z\",  // month end\n  \"2026-12-31T23:59:59.999Z\",  // year end\n  \"2026-03-08T07:30:00Z\",      // US DST switch\n])(\"creates an invoice at %s\", (iso) => {\n  const at = Date.parse(iso);\n  const inv = generateInvoice(user, opts, () => at);\n  expect(inv.createdAt).toBe(at);\n  // assert the period/due-date fields against fixed expected values\n});\n```\n\nIf changing the signature is too big a step right now, a smaller stopgap is `vi.useFakeTimers(); vi.setSystemTime(...)` in the tests plus `TZ=UTC` in the test script. That stops the flaking but leaves the code untestable at its seams.\n\n## Verify before committing\n\n1. Check whether L57 and L63 (and L92) feed related invoice fields. That's the straddling read the fix collapses into one.\n2. Check whether `billing.test.ts` builds expected values from its own clock.\n3. Reproduce the failure first: run the test with `vi.setSystemTime` at `2026-01-31T23:59:59.999Z` and with `TZ=America/Los_Angeles`. It should fail before the fix and pass after.\n\nSeparately, the scan warns that a suppression in `src/billing.ts` stopped matching after the 0.8.0 fingerprint change (it's listed as superseded). Check with `crimes migrate-pins --format json` whether it was silencing one of these findings.",
